WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
REDHAT:
Servers are something that most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:07.680
people never give much thought to. They’re simply&nbsp;
the background machines that make the modern world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.880
go round. But the world of servers is actually&nbsp;
far more fascinating than you might think. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:19.960
global server market is worth $90 billion and a&nbsp;
lot of household names serve the cloud and server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:26.320
market as well. This includes Amazon, Google,&nbsp;
Microsoft, Dell, Oracle, and Cisco. With this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.000
many big players, you would think that the server&nbsp;
market is cutthroat and extremely competitive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.520
especially given that their entire customer base&nbsp;
is other sophisticated companies as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.560
the general public. But what if I told you that&nbsp;
the server market has also been monopolized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:50.280
not by any of the common culprits like&nbsp;
Google or Amazon but actually IBM. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:58.040
96.3% of the world’s top web servers all&nbsp;
use the same OS Linux. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:04.120
even Windows and Android only have 70% market&nbsp;
share. Now, traditionally, Linux is an open source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:10.520
operating system that isn’t owned by any one&nbsp;
entity and is completely free to use. But this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:16.240
not the sort of Linux that companies use. Instead,&nbsp;
companies prefer to use an enterprise version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.640
Linux. There are a couple of popular distributions&nbsp;
in this market like Ubuntu, Mint, and Fedora. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:28.680
one distro rises above everyone else: Red Hat.&nbsp;
Red Hate Linux is basically the default choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.840
for companies and especially large companies.&nbsp;
In fact, over 90% of the Fortune 500 uses some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.680
sort of enterprise solution from Red Hat. For the&nbsp;
longest time, Red Hat was just a background shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.880
company that the general public had no clue about.&nbsp;
But, 4 years ago, IBM decided to change that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:55.880
purchasing the entirety of Red Hat for a cool $34&nbsp;
billion. So, now, it’s IBM that controls basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:03.240
the entire enterprise server OS market. But, how&nbsp;
did we even get here? How did one company end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:09.640
getting so much control over entire world’s tech&nbsp;
infrastructure? Well, join me, as we take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:24.382
back at the monumental rise of Red Hat and the&nbsp;
landmark deal that has put IBM back on the map.

00:02:24.382 --> 00:02:25.720
