00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:24.680
going back to RedHat, the story of RedHat dates&nbsp;
back 30 years to a bum named Robert Young. Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.240
had huge ambitions of running his own company&nbsp;
and making it big in the business world. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.200
the reality was that Robert was unemployed and&nbsp;
broke, working on “companies” in his wife’s sewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:42.120
closet. His latest endeavor was running a computer&nbsp;
supply catalog business. To get the word out about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.120
this business, Robert would regularly attend local&nbsp;
tech conferences and try to network with as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.920
people as possible. It was during one of these&nbsp;
networking events that Robert would run into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:58.760
man named Marc Ewing. Marc was your classic 1980s&nbsp;
geek software engineer. He spent his childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.320
summers attending computer camps and writing&nbsp;
programs for Apollo and Commodore computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.800
Within his local community and especially his&nbsp;
college community, he would naturally become known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.600
as the tech guy. Given that he liked wearing red&nbsp;
hats, people would begin referring to him as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.760
man in the red hat and the name stuck. Marc&nbsp;
wasn’t necessarily looking to start a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.840
company like Robert though. In fact, it seems like&nbsp;
he preferred the traditional path as he would turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:28.200
around and get a job at IBM after college. In his&nbsp;
free time though, he would continue meddling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.000
side projects like trying to customize Linux&nbsp;
for enterprise use. This project is what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.760
lead Marc to the fateful conference where he met&nbsp;
Robert. Robert would convince Marc to let him sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.320
Marc’s Linux distros through his new computer&nbsp;
supply catalog business and this marked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:49.560
beginning of a legendary partnership. At first, it&nbsp;
was simply a distribution deal, Robert would sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:55.480
Marc’s Linux CDs but Robert would end up selling&nbsp;
out of these CDs so often that he knew that Marc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:02.000
was onto something. So, in 1995, the two would&nbsp;
decide to join forces and create Red Hat Software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.920
with Robert serving as the CEO. Despite forming&nbsp;
a company and having profit incentive though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.640
the duo never really strayed away from their&nbsp;
roots. They didn’t just go out and file a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.680
of patents and trademarks. Instead, they continued&nbsp;
to support the open source community as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.800
believed that open collaboration was the best way&nbsp;
to create software. This turned out to be a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:29.040
decision not just from a community perspective but&nbsp;
from a reach perspective. Being open source gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:35.240
Red Hat quite a bit of notoriety within the tech&nbsp;
community leading to a lot more adoption. By 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.560
RedHat would grow to just over $10 million&nbsp;
in annual revenue. This was a solid 8 figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.280
business that was realistically worth $30 to $50&nbsp;
million but combine this with the dotcom bubble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:52.640
and things would get insane. They would launch the&nbsp;
IPO at a share price of $14 but by the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.000
first trading day itself, the share price would&nbsp;
rocket ot $52. And by the end of the second day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:05.040
the share price would rocket to $72, giving&nbsp;
the company a valuation of $4.8 billion. Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.960
Hat had gone from being a company that Robert&nbsp;
ran from his closet to a multi billion company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:17.360
within just a matter of years. It almost seemed to&nbsp;
good to be true and that’s because it was. Moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.600
into the 2000s, Red Hat would face an existential&nbsp;
crisis as the company came back down to reality.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:35.920
A HARSH REALITY CHECK:
The bottomline was that Red Hat was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:42.440
extremely overhyped and overvalued at least from&nbsp;
a stock perspective, and it’s no wonder why. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:48.640
investors saw Red Hat as the Windows of servers&nbsp;
and they weren’t wrong. Red Hat ended up becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:54.760
way more dominant than even Windows in terms of&nbsp;
market share, but there’s two major difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.640
First of all, Microsoft was already way bigger&nbsp;
than just Windows and two, there was a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:06.360
money to be made in the consumer market. And when&nbsp;
the market realized this, Red Hat stock would get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:12.440
annihilated down to just $4 per share. To make&nbsp;
things worse, both Robert and Marc would leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:18.840
Red Hat in 1999 and 2002 respectively. They didn’t&nbsp;
leave on bad terms or anything, they actually left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:24.840
on satisfied terms. Sure, they didn’t become&nbsp;
the next Bill Gates or Steve Jobs but they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.680
already made hundreds of millions each and they&nbsp;
were ready to enjoy life. You can’t really blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:35.520
them but they kind of did leave RedHat and all of&nbsp;
its employees out to dry. If all of this wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.680
bad enough, Red Hat would also start to see&nbsp;
new competitors pop up. SUSE Linux Enterprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:46.440
for example, came out in 2000. Ubuntu came out in&nbsp;
2004, and Oracle would follow up with their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:52.640
version in 2006. The value proposition of Red Hat&nbsp;
was starting to disappear despite them continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.840
to win a bunch of awards. For example, they won&nbsp;
the Operating System Product of the Year award 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:03.400
year in a row. They would also be ranked number&nbsp;
1 for value 2 years in a row. But, while tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:08.240
media continued to be nice to Red Hat, they knew&nbsp;
that they had to do something different if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.320
really wanted to stick around. They started&nbsp;
thinking about their distro less as a piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:18.040
of software and more as a solution because well&nbsp;
the software itself doesn’t give them a very big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:24.080
moat. Luckily for Red Hat, everyone was already&nbsp;
using their software, so the question wasn’t:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.520
how do we get more customers but rather, how do we&nbsp;
keep the customers that we already have. And from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.960
that perspective, the answer was easy: by offering&nbsp;
world class support. Support may not seem like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:41.760
big deal from a consumer perspective, but it’s&nbsp;
everything from an enterprise perspective. Keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.200
in mind, Red Hat is what’s keeping the entire tech&nbsp;
infrastructure of companies like Google and Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:53.960
up and running. And for companies at this scale,&nbsp;
even an hour of down time is tens of millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:59.480
dollars lost. So, keeping the backend in pristine&nbsp;
condition and getting immediate support when it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:05.240
not is of utmost importance. This is actually&nbsp;
quite ironic given that support was something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:10.520
that Red Hat initially neglected. In fact, in the&nbsp;
early days, Robert would actually joke about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:15.480
the only way to reach Red Hat support was through&nbsp;
email which was counterintuitive given that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:21.360
your computer needed support, you probably&nbsp;
couldn’t email. But moving into the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.840
Red Hat would approach support from a completely&nbsp;
differentlight. In fact, they would add a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.960
of pricing tiers to their software that were&nbsp;
directly linked to the level of support they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:38.520
came with. And let’s just say this gets pretty&nbsp;
expensive, as much as $2500 per license. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:43.680
with that being said, most large corporations&nbsp;
view this as a necessity and Red Hat very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.840
does live up to their price. They truly do offer&nbsp;
24x7 first class white glove tech support. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:56.000
easy to spin off another enterprise version of&nbsp;
Linux but matching the support and presence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:10.778
Red Hat is extremely difficult, and this made&nbsp;
way for a new wave of prosperity for Red Hat.

00:10:10.778 --> 00:10:12.480
RESURRECTED DOMINANCE:
After this change in approach, Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:18.880
Hat would return to setting record after record.&nbsp;
In 2009, they would be added to the S&amp;P 500 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.680
just 3 years later, they would become the first&nbsp;
open source company to reach a billion dollars in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:29.720
annual revenue. By 2015, they would reach $2&nbsp;
billion and by 2018, they would reach nearly $3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:35.840
billion. Red Hat was no longer the Dotcom boom&nbsp;
story that it was back in the 90s. By the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:42.240
2010s, Red Hat was a massive tech company offering&nbsp;
critical solutions to other massive companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.320
making billions in the process. In the business&nbsp;
world though, when a company reaches extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:52.920
heights, it’s only a matter of time until someone&nbsp;
else wants a piece of the pie. In the case of Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.920 --> 00:11:00.080
Hat, it was the worn down legacy giant: IBM. What&nbsp;
IBM really needed was a face lift, a new way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.880
appeal to and attract customers because almost no&nbsp;
modern company really considered IBM as a serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:12.720
solution. They all flocked to AWS, Azure, Google,&nbsp;
and even Oracle Cloud before they considered IBM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:19.200
So, IBM decided to target a different demographic,&nbsp;
a more legacy demographic. Companies like Anthem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:24.720
American Airlines, Honeywell, Panasonic, Deloitte,&nbsp;
and Exxon. According to Arvind Krishna, the CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:31.240
of IBM, most of these non tech companies are only&nbsp;
about 20% converted to cloud. So, the idea behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:37.520
buying RedHat for $34 billion is that this would&nbsp;
make IBM the one stop shop for legacy companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:43.480
who just want a simple straightforward reliable&nbsp;
solution. They don’t need the best performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:49.800
or the fastest response times or any of that. All&nbsp;
they want is a stress free migration to the cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:55.040
and that’s exactly what IBM is looking to offer.&nbsp;
Things have been working out decently well from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:01.040
IBM’s perspective, but many would argue that the&nbsp;
same cannot be said about Red Hat. It’s not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:06.200
IBM is making purposefully making bad choices&nbsp;
with RedHat or anything like that. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:10.560
you could argue that IBM is genuinely trying&nbsp;
their best to respect the opensource nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:16.800
RedHat while also trying to improve IBM cloud as&nbsp;
much as possible. As such, the problem seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.560
be much more holistic and qualitative. Many would&nbsp;
argue that the bureaucratic old school ways of IBM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:28.080
or simply rubbing off on RedHat and bringing down&nbsp;
the company. Much of RedHat’s leadership has left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:32.440
the company following the acquisition and the&nbsp;
people who are left frankly only care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:37.120
their own compensation. They don’t know the&nbsp;
users and they don’t care about the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:42.640
because they’ve never even used it. This has led&nbsp;
to some really unpopular decisions like pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:48.000
out unstable upstreams. With that being said&nbsp;
though, this does seem to be more of a case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.200
incompetence as opposed to any sort of malicious&nbsp;
intent. And as such, we have yet to see any sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:58.840
of large scale movement against Red Hat. But, the&nbsp;
vocal minority has already started raising several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:04.400
valid red flags, and if IBM continues running Red&nbsp;
Hat in the same manner, a shift away from Red Hat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:10.200
may very well be in the cards. So, the ball&nbsp;
is really in IBM’s court. They’ve somehow been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:16.120
able to covertly overtake an extremely powerful&nbsp;
piece of software, but will they actually be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:21.680
to maintain Red Hat’s ubiquity and dominance&nbsp;
or will they fumble the ball, only time will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:28.800
tell. If you’d like to take advantage of high&nbsp;
interest rates and have the government and top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:36.160
companies pay you 4-7%, check out our upcoming&nbsp;
bond investing app Silo in the description below.

